<resources>
    <!--
         Declares the fingerprint reader.

         The vendor HAL is there and running (init.svc.vendor.fps_hal =
         running, and
         android.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint@2.1::IBiometricsFingerprint is
         registered), the android.hardware.fingerprint feature is declared and
         the system service exists. Only this list is missing, which in AOSP is
         empty: without it AuthService registers no authenticator and the
         service repeats

           E FingerprintService: No providers found
           W FingerprintService: Null provider for isHardwareDetectedDeprecated

         on every request, so nothing shows up in Settings.

         The format is ID:Modality:Strength, as per the comment in
         frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml: here sensor 0,
         modality 2 (fingerprint), strength 15 (strong).
    -->
    <string-array name="config_biometric_sensors" translatable="false">
        <item>0:2:15</item>
    </string-array>

    <!--
         Enables WiFi Display (Miracast).

         AOSP keeps it off by default and asks for two things: the
         "audio.r_submix.default" audio module, which is here
         (/vendor/lib64/hw/), and vendor support, declared with
         ro.vendor.mtk_wfd_support=1. Both verified on the device.

         It is the only video output this phone can have: HDMI is not even
         compiled into the kernel (CONFIG_MTK_HDMI_SUPPORT is not set) and the
         USB-C port declares only the "ufp dfp" data roles, with no alt mode.
    -->
    <bool name="config_enableWifiDisplay">true</bool>

    <!--
      Haptic feedback durations, measured on this phone.

      The vibrator HAL (device/doogee/s88pro/vibrator) supports no prebaked
      effects, so keyboard taps, button clicks and long presses are played
      through these fallback patterns: CLICK uses config_virtualKeyVibePattern,
      TICK config_clockTickVibePattern, HEAVY_CLICK config_longPressVibePattern
      and DOUBLE_CLICK config_doubleClickVibePattern
      (VibrationSettings.createEffectsFromResource).

      Why the keyboard went silent. The stock Android 10 framework-res gave
      keyboard taps a pattern of their own, config_keyboardTapVibePattern =
      [40]. This framework no longer has that resource: KEYBOARD_TAP becomes
      EFFECT_CLICK with fallback (HapticFeedbackVibrationProvider
      .getKeyboardVibration), and the AOSP fallback for EFFECT_CLICK is
      [0, 20]. The driver stretches anything between vib_limit and vib_timer,
      9 and 25 ms in the device tree, up to 25 ms, so a keypress went from
      40 ms of drive to 25. That is below what this motor can render.

      The motor is an eccentric rotating mass in a heavy rugged body. Measured
      by writing /sys/class/leds/vibrator directly, at typing cadence: four
      pulses 300 ms apart, a finger resting on the glass, the person holding
      the phone reporting:

          20 ms (25 after the driver)   too weak
          30 ms                         borderline
          40 ms                         felt, chosen
          120 ms                        felt, too insistent for typing

      The context matters a great deal: with the phone simply held and no
      finger on the screen, nothing under 120 ms was felt at all, and an
      earlier version of this overlay was calibrated on that and shipped 120 ms
      everywhere; it was felt, and it buzzed. The keyboard is used with a
      finger on the glass, and that is the threshold that counts.

      The power side was checked too: sampling the PMIC while pulsing, the VIBR
      LDO comes up with no measurable delay and stays on for the length asked,
      at 2.8 V (VOSEL 9, the stock device tree value).

      TICK equals CLICK on purpose: below the threshold a lighter tick is not
      lighter, it is absent. HEAVY_CLICK is longer so a long press stays
      distinguishable from a tap. The gap in the double click lets the mass
      coast down, or the two pulses merge into one. KEYBOARD_RELEASE asks for
      TICK without fallback and is skipped by the framework on a vibrator with
      no effects, which is what it should do.
    -->
    <integer-array name="config_virtualKeyVibePattern" translatable="false">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>40</item>
    </integer-array>

    <integer-array name="config_clockTickVibePattern" translatable="false">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>40</item>
    </integer-array>

    <integer-array name="config_longPressVibePattern" translatable="false">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>100</item>
    </integer-array>

    <integer-array name="config_doubleClickVibePattern" translatable="false">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>40</item>
        <item>100</item>
        <item>40</item>
    </integer-array>
</resources>
